.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/tiny-invariant Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "tiny-invariant 🔬💥"
.PP
\fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP  \fR\fP \fR\fP

.PP
A tiny \fR\fRinvariant\fP\fP alternative\&.
.SH "What is \fRinvariant\fP?"
.PP
An \fRinvariant\fP function takes a value, and if the value is \fRfalsy\fP then the \fRinvariant\fP function will throw\&. If the value is \fRtruthy\fP, then the function will not throw\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import invariant from 'tiny\-invariant';

invariant(truthyValue, 'This should not throw!');

invariant(falsyValue, 'This will throw!');
// Error('Invariant violation: This will throw!');
.fi
.PP

.PP
You can also provide a function to generate your message, for when your message is expensive to create

.PP
.PP
.nf
import invariant from 'tiny\-invariant';

invariant(value, () => getExpensiveMessage());
.fi
.PP
.SH "Why \fRtiny-invariant\fP?"
.PP
The \fR\fRlibrary: invariant\fP\fP supports passing in arguments to the \fRinvariant\fP function in a sprintf style \fR(condition, format, a, b, c, d, e, f)\fP\&. It has internal logic to execute the sprintf substitutions\&. The sprintf logic is not removed in production builds\&. \fRtiny-invariant\fP has dropped all of the sprintf logic\&. \fRtiny-invariant\fP allows you to pass a single string message\&. With \fRtemplate literals\fP there is really no need for a custom message formatter to be built into the library\&. If you need a multi part message you can just do this:

.PP
.PP
.nf
invariant(condition, `Hello, ${name} \- how are you today?`);
.fi
.PP
.SH "Type narrowing"
.PP
\fRtiny-invariant\fP is useful for correctly narrowing types for \fRflow\fP and \fRtypescript\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const value: Person | null = { name: 'Alex' }; // type of value == 'Person | null'
invariant(value, 'Expected value to be a person');
// type of value has been narrowed to 'Person'
.fi
.PP
.SH "API: \fR(condition: any, message?: string | (() => string)) => void\fP"
.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRcondition\fP is required and can be anything
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRmessage\fP optional \fRstring\fP or a function that returns a \fRstring\fP (\fR() => string\fP)
.PP
.SH "Installation"
.PP
.PP
.nf
# yarn
yarn add tiny\-invariant

# npm
npm install tiny\-invariant \-\-save
.fi
.PP
.SH "Dropping your \fRmessage\fP for kb savings!"
.PP
Big idea: you will want your compiler to convert this code:

.PP
.PP
.nf
invariant(condition, 'My cool message that takes up a lot of kbs');
.fi
.PP

.PP
Into this:

.PP
.PP
.nf
if (!condition) {
  if ('production' !== process\&.env\&.NODE_ENV) {
    invariant(false, 'My cool message that takes up a lot of kbs');
  } else {
    invariant(false);
  }
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBabel\fP: recommend \fR\fRbabel-plugin-dev-expression\fP\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBTypeScript\fP: recommend \fR\fRtsdx\fP\fP (or you can run \fRbabel-plugin-dev-expression\fP after TypeScript compiling)
.PP

.PP
Your bundler can then drop the code in the \fR"production" !== process\&.env\&.NODE_ENV\fP block for your production builds to end up with this:

.PP
.PP
.nf
if (!condition) {
  invariant(false);
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
rollup: use \fRrollup-plugin-replace\fP and set \fRNODE_ENV\fP to \fRproduction\fP and then \fRrollup\fP will treeshake out the unused code
.IP "\(bu" 2
Webpack: \fRinstructions\fP
.PP
.SH "Builds"
.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
We have a \fRes\fP (EcmaScript module) build
.IP "\(bu" 2
We have a \fRcjs\fP (CommonJS) build
.IP "\(bu" 2
We have a \fRumd\fP (Universal module definition) build in case you needed it
.PP

.PP
We expect \fRprocess\&.env\&.NODE_ENV\fP to be available at module compilation\&. We cache this value
.SH "That's it!"
.PP
🤘 
